# LoRa plane configuration template - MULE
#
# The Meshtastic daemon and the supervisor around it. FML-ADR-026 makes this a
# separate NON-IP plane and the degraded-mode lifeline: what a team falls back
# to when every other bearer has gone.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Every value below is TBD. This file describes what must be
# configured and what the supervisor must guarantee. It is an input to
# configuration generation, not a file to be edited on a node.
#
# Trades: TBR-RF-02 (band, channel, EIRP, duty cycle, coexistence with HaLow)
#         TBR-NET-02 (addressing behind the node; open, and see below)
#         TBR-TAK-01 (what is worth carrying on this bearer at all)
#
# NOTHING HERE IS AN IP CONCERN. No address, no bridge, no route, no place in
# nftables' forwarding rules, and nothing for systemd-networkd to own. If a
# change to this file starts needing an IP address, that is a change request
# against FML-ADR-026 and not an implementation detail.

# --- Radio attachment -------------------------------------------------------
# FML-ADR-026: "The radio attaches to the host through an approved standard
# interface: USB serial, UART, or TCP where the radio provides it."
#
# attachment=TBD               # serial | uart | tcp
# device=TBD                   # e.g. /dev/serial/by-id/... . Do NOT use a
#                              # kernel-assigned name like /dev/ttyACM0: it is
#                              # allocation order, not identity. A by-id path
#                              # is stable across reboots and across a second
#                              # adapter being plugged in.
#
# The hardware is not selected. What has been observed on a bench is that a
# USB LoRa adapter enumerates as a CDC-ACM serial device owned by root:dialout,
# which is what makes the deployment question below a real one rather than a
# formality. See docs/evidence/TBR-RF-02/2026-08-30-sx1262-usb-modem-identified.md.

# --- Region-dependent values are NOT here -----------------------------------
# Band, channel, transmit power and duty cycle come from the region profile in
# regions/<region-id>/ and are resolved by tools/gen-config.py, which already
# reads lora.default_channel, lora.max_eirp_dbm, lora.band_low_hz and
# lora.band_high_hz. They are all TBD in every profile.
#
# This matters more on this bearer than on the others. 902-928 MHz exists in
# the United States and not in the EU or UK, which use 863-868 MHz with
# duty-cycle limits that have no US analogue. A node that transmits on a band
# its region does not permit is a regulatory problem, not a bug. See
# REGULATORY.md and regions/README.md.

# --- The supervisor ---------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THING THIS FILE EXISTS TO STATE.
#
# .github/workflows/lora-probe.yml established, at the cost of a run, that
# changing a node's configuration REBOOTS meshtasticd, and that in the
# container image the re-exec fails and the process exits:
#
#   [Router] Reboot in 7 seconds
#   Rebooting
#   execv() returned -1!
#   error: No such file or directory
#
# So a configuration push can leave the lifeline bearer dead, silently, on the
# plane a team reaches for when everything else has failed. That is the worst
# failure this system has and it is not hypothetical: it happened, twice, to
# the probe.
#
# The supervisor SHALL restart the daemon when it exits, and SHALL NOT treat a
# configuration push as complete until the daemon answers its API again. The
# probe waits for the API rather than sleeping a guessed interval, for exactly
# this reason, and anything driving a node has to do the same.
#
# restart_policy=TBD           # must restart on exit
# api_ready_timeout_s=TBD      # how long to wait before calling a push failed
#
# A node whose LoRa daemon is not answering has no lifeline. mule/bringup.py
# and the LoRaPlane interface both read that state rather than assuming it, and
# mule/status.py reports lora_available False when the stack does not answer or
# cannot be asked.

# --- How it is deployed is NOT decided --------------------------------------
# FML-ADR-029 makes rootless Podman quadlets the default, and carves this case
# out in the same sentence:
#
#   "Rootless containers shall be the default for ordinary browser
#    applications, file services, status services and other workloads that do
#    not require privileged hardware or host networking access."
#
# meshtasticd requires a serial device. It is therefore outside that default by
# the ADR's own terms, and nothing has decided what replaces it: a privileged
# quadlet with the device passed through, a native package with a systemd unit,
# or something else.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT DECIDE IT. The choice interacts with the image build,
# which does not exist, and with whether the daemon comes from a distribution
# package or an OCI image, which FML-ADR-048 has not settled either. Whoever
# needs it opens an ADR; see docs/ROADMAP-DEV.md item 1.1 step 4.

# --- Addressing behind the node is not implemented ---------------------------
# TBR-NET-02's specification selects a one-byte member tag carried inside the
# Meshtastic payload, so that a message can reach a person behind a MULE rather
# than only the MULE. It is measured as carriable: the transport moves a tag
# byte intact, and the usable payload is 231 bytes rather than the 233 the
# protobuf constant reports. See
# docs/evidence/TBR-NET-02/2026-08-30-tag-payload-measurement.md.
#
# It is NOT implemented, and no value here relates to it. Until it is, a team
# behind one MULE is one address on this plane, and person-to-person messaging
# degrades to MULE-to-MULE at CONOPS section 50.8 with nothing telling the
# operator so. The gateway that would carry the tag is
# services/gateways/, which is blocked.
